findings.claim:
Route 3 sends items 3, 113 and 171 into AutomorphicFormsOnReductiveGroups AF.0, AF.2 and AF.3, but none of these layers plans what the items state.
- AF.0 plans test functions and growth.
- AF.2 plans automorphic spaces, finiteness and factorization.
- AF.3 plans constant terms along proper parabolics and cusp forms.
The items need:
- (a) the non-constant Fourier–Whittaker coefficients W_{φ,ξ} and the expansion (1.12)–(1.13);
- (b) the vanishing criterion of Lemma 13.6 (item 113);
- (c) generation of SL2 by unipotent subgroups, over any field and over a local field (item 171), which is group theory;
- (d) the Q-structures on A_hol given by q-expansion (part of item 3).
AutomorphicBundles:B5 plans (d): q-expansions at Hilbert cusps and the expansion principle. No stage plans (a)–(c) as general statements. A source route is for items that belong inside existing layers, so these items are left unowned. Also: Item 3 bundles the space A_exp(H(A), K, k) with the holomorphic spaces and sends all of it, by source route 3, to AutomorphicFormsOnReductiveGroups AF.0/AF.2/AF.3. A_exp is defined only for F0 = Q and consists of smooth weight-k functions of at worst exponential growth whose image under the lowering operator ½(i 1; 1 −i) lies in A_hol(H(A), K, k−2). Those layers plan moderate-growth automorphic forms, automorphic representations and constant terms. They do not plan exponential growth or the lowering-operator condition, and route 3's reason mentions only the holomorphic spaces and Fourier uniqueness. A_exp is a working space of the AFL proof: Theorem 8.4, Corollary 10.4 and Proposition 14.5 (items 72, 88 and 121, all in route 11) assert membership in it.

The proof of Lemma B.2(i) (item 137) uses two standard commutative-algebra results that no item records:
- the Koszul complex of a regular sequence is a free resolution;
- in a Cohen–Macaulay local ring, a sequence that cuts the dimension by its length is regular.
Route 10's brief delegates them to 'its common commutative-algebra/derived owner' but names no roadmap, although PROTOCOL §16 requires imports by title and id. No such owner exists:
- Mathlib at 082e2d3 has no Koszul complex and no Cohen–Macaulay rings;
- item 142 says the regular-sequence predicate 'is not the Koszul resolution';
- the only atlas stage that plans Koszul complexes is IntegralHeckeAndGaloisDeterminants:IHG.6, for its own Ribet-theory use. Also: Route 10's brief imports no owner for the formal schemes it builds on, and it names no owner for the commutative algebra behind Lemma B.2. The formal schemes are noetherian formal schemes, coherent sheaves on them, closed formal subschemes, the affine description and properness (EGA I §10 and EGA III, cited [15], [16]). The brief says 'Build noetherian formal schemes' … categories' but imports only SchemeKTheoryOperations, TauCeti.ExactK0 and SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.5. The atlas plans formal schemes in AdicSpacesPartII:F0 and SchemeAndStackFoundations:SF.4, and neither library has them. Lemma B.2's proof needs two facts: the Koszul complex of a regular sequence is a free resolution, and in a Cohen–Macaulay local ring elements cutting the dimension by m form a regular sequence. Neither is an item. The brief sends them to an unnamed 'common commutative-algebra/derived owner', whereas a brief must name its imports by title and id. Item 142 covers only the predicate IsRegular.

